WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
If you look closely at the reflection here, you'll see a familiar, exhausting loop.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Running Gradle Tasks, building release bundles, opening a browser, logging into the play console,

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.000
dragging files into a webinar face.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Doing this once is a chore.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Doing it for every single bug fix or feature update creates a massive drain on your time.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
We are going to replace that entire manual sequence with one automated command that you run straight from your terminal.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
That tool is fastling.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
This maps out exactly how React Native divides deployment labor.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
The JavaScript side routes to tools like Metro and EAS.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Fastlane strictly handles the native Android architecture.

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.000
It compiles Java and Kotlin code, builds the bundle, and handles the store upload.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Before we start writing the automation, you need one specific file ready, your Google Play service account JSON file.

13
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
This is the cryptographic key that gives your terminal permission to talk to the play star.

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Once you have that JSON file, we can begin.

15
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Configuring this tool is how you transition from repeating the same manual steps to defining those steps once as a script.

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Step 1. Open your project terminal.

17
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Take a look at this project file tree because this is where most developers make their first mistake.

18
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Do not run the initialization command at the root of your React Native project.

19
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Fastlane needs to integrate with the native build tools directly.

20
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Instead, explicitly change your directory into the Android folder by typing CD Android.

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Inside the Android directory, run Fastlane in it.

22
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Watch the file tree as that executes.

23
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Fastlane generates a new directory alongside your native source files.

24
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Inside that new folder, you'll find the two core configuration files we need to edit, the app file and the fast file.

25
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Each serves a distinct purpose.

26
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.000
The app file handles your authorization to Google.

27
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The fast file contains the actual workflow steps you want to execute.

28
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
By forcing initialization inside the native directory, Fastlane bypasses the JavaScript wrapper.

29
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.000
It is now positioned to directly manipulate the underlying Android build system.

30
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Let's start with authorization by opening the app file.

31
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
This document acts as your project's secure passport to the Google Play Store.

32
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Looking at this editor window, we need to provide two pieces of information.

33
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
First, set the package name variable to match your app's exact package identifier.

34
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Second, map the JSON key file variable to the relative path of the Google Play service account file we secured earlier.

35
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.000
That path must be strictly accurate relative to the folder where you run Fastlane.

36
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Critically, you must immediately add that JSON file to your .get ignore.

37
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
If you commit that key to a public repository, anyone can push updates to your app.

38
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
With the path mapped and the file secured, your app file now proves authorization to Google,

39
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.000
without ever exposing your private keys to your team or the public.

40
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.000
With authorization handled, open the fast file.

41
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.000
This is where we script the automation logic itself.

42
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Fastlane operates using what it calls lanes.

43
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Think of a lane exactly like this high-speed mechanical assembly line.

44
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:12.000
It is a sequenced, repeatable workflow that groups multiple discrete actions together into one continuous process.

45
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Your initialized fast file will be full of example boilerplate.

46
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Delete it all and let's draft a clean custom deployment lane from scratch.

47
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Here is the code block for our new lane.

48
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Inside, the first component we add tells Fastlane to trigger the underlying gradle tool to compile a release bundle.

49
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Directly beneath that, we add the second step using the native action upload to Play Store.

50
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:41.000
The second line intercepts the bundle that was just compiled and securely pushes it through the API directly to the Play Console.

51
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
By writing this out, you can solidate those manual terminal commands into a single blueprint.

52
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Instead of relying on your memory every time you deploy, you're relying on a file that performs the exact same sequence every time it's called.

53
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Now, return to your terminal and execute your newly minted custom lane.

54
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
As the script runs, you'll see the terminal trace the exact sequence you built.

55
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:10.000
And when it finishes, you get a summary table with green check marks confirming a successful build and a completed Play Store upload.

56
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.000
The browser is no longer part of your deployment loop.

57
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Every step from compilation to store submission now happens directly through this connection.

58
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
But look at this network view for a second.

59
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.000
As effective as this lane is, it still relies on your local machine and your manual trigger to start the process.

60
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:35.000
However, this localized setup is the exact foundation required for a full continuous integration pipeline.

61
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Next, we will connect this Fastlane configuration to GitHub actions, allowing your code pushes to trigger this entire sequence from the cloud automatically.

